given to us. So we've got a really broad range of interesting themes that have come

51
00:04:06,120 --> 00:04:11,250
out for this conference. So we hope you enjoy this smorgasbord of tasty conference

52
00:04:11,340 --> 00:04:12,870
bites. And we're going to

53
00:04:14,820 --> 00:04:18,781
start the session with the foundations that the field is built on, the literature.

54
00:04:19,560 --> 00:04:22,890
So several of the speakers today have already shown us excerpts of letters,

55
00:04:23,100 --> 00:04:26,970
digitized books, and journals, and there'll be a strong set of symposia and

56
00:04:27,031 --> 00:04:29,730
workshops related to literature throughout the conference.

57
00:04:30,420 --> 00:04:34,591
Our first speaker, Nicole Carney, will remind us of the importance of literature

58
00:04:34,650 --> 00:04:38,341
with her talk about the Biodiversity Heritage Library. Thanks, Nicole.

59
00:04:46,230 --> 00:04:48,960
Thank you, Ellie. Hello, everyone.

60
00:04:49,020 --> 00:04:54,031
It is a great honor to be speaking in this beautiful room at TADWIG in what is an

61
00:04:54,060 --> 00:04:57,420
incredibly significant year for the Biodiversity Heritage Library.

62
00:04:58,080 --> 00:05:01,531
Those of you who have been following along will know that we have been on a

63
00:05:01,620 --> 00:05:03,960
precarious and uncertain journey.

64
00:05:04,470 --> 00:05:08,250
Last year, we were thrown into the wilderness, and we have had to find our own way

65
00:05:08,490 --> 00:05:14,070
out. This beautiful illustration is from Joseph Hooker's 1854 Himalayan

66
00:05:14,160 --> 00:05:19,201
journals. It's a living bridge. It is formed from the aerial roots of fig trees,

67
00:05:19,560 --> 00:05:22,860
and for me, it really captures where BHL is right now.

68
00:05:23,580 --> 00:05:27,091
For the past 18 months, we've been building a new bridge for BHL.

69
00:05:27,420 --> 00:05:31,980
It is holding us up for now. We've put down some strong roots, but there are gaps.

70
00:05:32,341 --> 00:05:36,990
BHL could still fall, and we don't yet know what is on the other side, which is

71
00:05:37,020 --> 00:05:41,730
both scary and exciting. Today, I'm going to tell you the story of how we've got

72
00:05:41,790 --> 00:05:45,420
this far and what it still might take to get to the other side.

73
00:05:46,740 --> 00:05:50,400
Now, I know most in the room are very familiar with BHL because it is essential to

74
00:05:50,460 --> 00:05:54,630
your work. But for those who aren't, the Biodiversity Heritage Library is the

75
00:05:54,690 --> 00:05:59,430
world's largest virtual library of open access biodiversity literature and archival

76
00:05:59,490 --> 00:06:03,900
material, and one of the world's most critical resources of biodiversity knowledge.

77
00:06:05,520 --> 00:06:11,220
It is a global consortium of 684 contributing organizations and libraries who have

78
00:06:11,250 --> 00:06:15,720
together made over 64 million pages of biodiversity knowledge freely accessible

79
00:06:15,810 --> 00:06:20,610
online. It contains information on millions of the world's species and is the

80
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:23,730
foundation of our understanding of biodiversity.

81
00:06:24,750 --> 00:06:28,800
BHL exists because the cultivation of natural science cannot be efficiently carried

82
00:06:28,860 --> 00:06:33,510
on without reference to an extensive library, and the extensive library on BHL

83
00:06:33,570 --> 00:06:38,880
contains information and resources produced as early as the 1100s and as recently

84
00:06:38,970 --> 00:06:39,930
as last month.

85
00:06:41,130 --> 00:06:45,000
Every page is fully searchable. Our taxonomic name recognition allows you to search

86
00:06:45,030 --> 00:06:47,370
for both current and past scientific names.

87
00:06:48,870 --> 00:06:54,690
We have added article data to over 450 scientific articles, and over 80,000

88
00:06:54,780 --> 00:06:58,831
of those now have DOIs, bringing those articles into the modern linked network of

89
00:06:58,890 --> 00:07:03,870
scholarly research. We retrospectively assign DOIs to species descriptions

90
00:07:03,901 --> 00:07:07,201
published centuries ago, all the way through to the current day.

91
00:07:07,320 --> 00:07:11,311
This is a DOI BHL assigned to a species description that was published in November

92
00:07:11,370 --> 00:07:16,410
last year. To give you a sense of the massive amount of information made accessible

93
00:07:16,470 --> 00:07:20,160
and discoverable on the Biodiversity Heritage Library, this is a map produced by

94
00:07:20,190 --> 00:07:25,530
Rod Page. Every single dot on this map represents a latitude and a longitude pair

95
00:07:25,591 --> 00:07:27,420
in a scholarly article on BHL.

96
00:07:28,980 --> 00:07:34,380
However, this important discoverability work and our work assigning DOIs and many

97
00:07:34,440 --> 00:07:39,420
other BHL projects were put on hold last year when we were thrown into an emergency

98
00:07:39,480 --> 00:07:44,220
mode, when we received the unexpected news that the Smithsonian Institution, our

99
00:07:44,280 --> 00:07:48,540
administrative and technical home, would cease to host the BHL on the 1st of

100
00:07:48,630 --> 00:07:54,420
January this year. This decision placed BHL, one of the world's most widely used

101
00:07:54,480 --> 00:07:57,660
and longest-running biodiversity infrastructures, at risk.

102
00:07:58,170 --> 00:08:02,940
We were completely at sea, with the very real possibility of being pulled under.

103
00:08:04,800 --> 00:08:08,790
As our host, the Smithsonian had provided the BHL secretariat staff and the

104
00:08:08,850 --> 00:08:13,530
technical team, as well as the seven servers required to run the BHL website and

105
00:08:13,561 --> 00:08:18,750
the website itself. With this support gone, we had to act quickly if we were to

106
00:08:18,841 --> 00:08:23,970
help keep BHL afloat. We formed an emergency and transition team, and together with

107
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:28,500
the BHL Executive Committee, we have worked tirelessly over the past 18 months to

108
00:08:28,561 --> 00:08:32,100
build a more autonomous, global, and sustainable BHL.

109
00:08:33,480 --> 00:08:36,780
At this point, back in April last year, we had no idea what we were going to do,

110
00:08:37,020 --> 00:08:40,980
how we would gather the support we needed to continue, or what the future of BHL

111
00:08:41,010 --> 00:08:45,330
might look like. So it was more important than ever for us to communicate openly

112
00:08:45,390 --> 00:08:49,140
and honestly with our community, to say that we didn't yet know what we were going

113
00:08:49,140 --> 00:08:54,030
to do, we didn't have the answers, to invite their ideas and support, and to

114
00:08:54,090 --> 00:08:58,380
reassure them that we would do everything we could to save the BHL and its staff.

115
00:08:59,370 --> 00:09:03,090
We communicated every stage of the transition across our blog and on social media,

116
00:09:03,510 --> 00:09:08,431
and we started a community forum on Discourse to invite their ideas and concerns.

117
00:09:09,504 --> 00:09:15,474
The threat to the BHL encouraged an overwhelming outpouring of appreciation

118
00:09:15,534 --> 00:09:21,234
and panic from our users. These included comments like, "BHL is one of the most

119
00:09:21,264 --> 00:09:25,344
important, most valuable online projects and one of the greatest collections on the

120
00:09:25,404 --> 00:09:30,024
internet. It is an indispensable record of the earth's biodiversity and represents

121
00:09:30,054 --> 00:09:33,084
an invaluable part of our scientific and cultural heritage.

122
00:09:33,504 --> 00:09:38,034
It's an absolutely vital resource for ecologists and evolutionary biologists." This

123
00:09:38,064 --> 00:09:40,104
was my favorite, a pledge from a user.

124
00:09:40,464 --> 00:09:44,724
She says, "I will send a box of handmade cookies every year for the rest of my life

125
00:09:44,754 --> 00:09:47,334
to whichever institution takes on the BHL."

126
00:09:49,464 --> 00:09:54,384
In June last year, we opened a global call for support, inviting expressions of

127
00:09:54,474 --> 00:09:59,184
interest from institutions and stakeholders to host BHL's operational and technical

128
00:09:59,274 --> 00:10:04,524
roles and services. In August, we moved to a new financial and legal home, the

129
00:10:04,584 --> 00:10:06,834
Council on Library and Information Resources.

130
00:10:07,224 --> 00:10:12,205
This means that BHL is now independently able to accept donations, pursue grants,

131
00:10:12,504 --> 00:10:16,944
and manage our own staff contracts. It also provide BHL with autonomy.

132
00:10:17,514 --> 00:10:23,184
BHL is no longer constrained by a single US federally affiliated

133
00:10:23,244 --> 00:10:27,084
university, no longer constrained by their requirements and shifting priorities.

134
00:10:27,534 --> 00:10:31,284
We're now operating as an independent, globally governed consortium.

135
00:10:32,484 --> 00:10:36,624
BHL's call for support invited offers of technical hosting,

136
00:10:37,974 --> 00:10:41,034
support for our existing systems, and some start funding.

137
00:10:41,094 --> 00:10:44,545
What we received is giving us stability for one to two years.

138
00:10:44,664 --> 00:10:50,304
I want to really emphasize this. BHL is secure and only secure

139
00:10:50,394 --> 00:10:52,014
through to 2027.

140
00:10:54,354 --> 00:10:58,764
On the 1st of January, BHL officially moved across to its new independent chapter.

141
00:10:59,334 --> 00:11:02,994
On the left is our blog post explaining what that means for BHL and its staff, and

142
00:11:03,024 --> 00:11:06,774
in February, we shared the exciting news that we have secured the staff positions

143
00:11:06,834 --> 00:11:08,514
of all existing staff.

144
00:11:10,134 --> 00:11:13,705
During this time, we have been extremely grateful to our BHL members.

145
00:11:13,914 --> 00:11:18,534
These are the organizations who govern BHL and whose membership fees, along with

146
00:11:18,564 --> 00:11:22,795
those of our affiliates, provide BHL with a reliable source of support.

147
00:11:23,274 --> 00:11:27,054
Three of those organizations, the University of South Florida, Vanderbilt

148
00:11:27,144 --> 00:11:31,795
University, and Zenberg Nature Support, joined BHL during the transition.

149
00:11:32,154 --> 00:11:37,314
At the most uncertain time in BHL's history, these organizations decided that we

150
00:11:37,344 --> 00:11:39,295
were worth supporting and joining.

151
00:11:40,584 --> 00:11:44,455
Two of those organizations are providing BHL with significant technical support.

152
00:11:44,814 --> 00:11:48,924
The University of South Florida Libraries are providing significant system support

153
00:11:48,984 --> 00:11:49,614
from the core,

154
00:11:50,904 --> 00:11:55,134
BHL's metadata collection workflow system, for three years, while Vanderbilt

155
00:11:55,194 --> 00:11:58,404
University is providing us with an associate cloud engineer.

156
00:11:58,825 --> 00:12:03,834
Rahul Sharma joins the technical team in BHL in February, and his role will help

157
00:12:03,894 --> 00:12:07,194
build a resilient modern technical foundation for BHL.

158
00:12:08,004 --> 00:12:11,844
But the news that most excited our users was that the Field Museum would take over

159
00:12:11,904 --> 00:12:15,504
the hosting of BHL's website and technical infrastructure.

160
00:12:16,045 --> 00:12:19,554
This was the news that answered the question that was most asked by our users

161
00:12:19,584 --> 00:12:23,033
during the transition, which was, what is going to happen to the BHL website?

162
00:12:25,104 --> 00:12:29,124
The response was an outpouring of relief and gratitude from across the world.

163
00:12:29,575 --> 00:12:34,914
It was the best news. That the incredible free, open treasure trove of nature and

164
00:12:35,124 --> 00:12:38,664
the crown jewel of biodiversity literature had been saved.

165
00:12:39,084 --> 00:12:42,325
And from my favorite user, "I now know where to send cookies."

166
00:12:44,934 --> 00:12:49,284
That big move from Washington, DC, to Chicago was completed at the end of June and

167
00:12:49,344 --> 00:12:52,734
marked the final piece of our transition away from the Smithsonian.

168
00:12:54,054 --> 00:12:58,765
With BHL's immediate future becoming more stable, we were finally able to start

169
00:12:58,825 --> 00:13:03,024
thinking beyond survival to the future of BHL's aging platform.

170
00:13:04,164 --> 00:13:08,484
Critical part of this process was to ask our users what they most wanted improved.

171
00:13:08,994 --> 00:13:12,445
In July, we ran a BHL user survey, and the message was clear.

172
00:13:13,075 --> 00:13:17,904
Enhanced text search was our users' number one priority, followed by image search

173
00:13:17,964 --> 00:13:19,254
and an improved page viewer.

174
00:13:20,515 --> 00:13:25,104
Our BHL platform modernization project is now underway, and it's much more than a

175
00:13:25,164 --> 00:13:30,924
technical upgrade. The goal is to replace BHL's legacy infrastructure with a modern

176
00:13:30,984 --> 00:13:35,184
cloud-based platform that has better search and discovery, improved performance and

177
00:13:35,244 --> 00:13:40,704
reliability, and data that is connected, machine actionable, and easier to find,

178
00:13:40,765 --> 00:13:45,684
reuse, and connect through better APIs, linked data, and persistent identifiers.

179
00:13:46,585 --> 00:13:50,335
Alongside this work, we have some really exciting new collaborations underway,

180
00:13:50,454 --> 00:13:54,265
including a major project led by the Natural History Museum in London.

181
00:13:54,774 --> 00:13:59,874
The NHM team is developing new ways to unlock biodiversity literature at scale, and

182
00:13:59,904 --> 00:14:03,774
they're in the final stages of recruiting a data scientist dedicated to this

183
00:14:03,835 --> 00:14:08,244
project. Here is who will be speaking more about this exciting work tomorrow in our

184
00:14:08,274 --> 00:14:10,795
BHL symposium. Encourage you to come along.

185
00:14:11,934 --> 00:14:15,714
We've also expanded the BHL executive committee, adding a strategic development

186
00:14:15,804 --> 00:14:20,484
officer and a member at large, which will increase our capacity for fundraising,

187
00:14:20,634 --> 00:14:22,434
partnerships, and long-term planning.

188
00:14:23,214 --> 00:14:27,864
David Eagleman, our chair, and our new strategic development officer, Amanda Bozar,

189
00:14:27,924 --> 00:14:30,954
are both here at tADWIG this week. Please do go and talk to them.

190
00:14:32,604 --> 00:14:36,924
Building this new future has also made us realize that BHL needs a more diverse and

191
00:14:36,984 --> 00:14:42,504
resilient funding base. At the end of 2025, we ran a targeted campaign called Give

192
00:14:42,564 --> 00:14:47,634
BHL Wings, calling on our users to help BHL take flight by the 1st of January.

193
00:14:48,174 --> 00:14:53,004
We set a target of $50,000 US, and we reached that goal on New Year's Eve, which

194
00:14:53,064 --> 00:14:54,265
totally made my year.

195
00:14:55,825 --> 00:15:00,204
Our plea also reached the media. In June this year, The Guardian published a major

196
00:15:00,265 --> 00:15:05,244
feature on BHL, bringing BHL and the very real threat to its future to a much wider

197
00:15:05,335 --> 00:15:10,045
audience. We saw an immediate spike in our public donations, which brought in more

198
00:15:10,075 --> 00:15:13,765
than $10,000 US on the day that article was published.

199
00:15:14,454 --> 00:15:19,945
We were blown away by messages of support and calls to save the extraordinary BHL.

200
00:15:20,664 --> 00:15:24,114
One reader suggested that if you're a tech billionaire or even just a millionaire,

201
00:15:24,474 --> 00:15:25,825
you could do something extraordinary.

202
00:15:25,914 --> 00:15:27,954
So if you do know one, please send them our way.

203
00:15:29,545 --> 00:15:32,664
BHL had never had to rely on public fundraising before this.

204
00:15:33,054 --> 00:15:35,423
Now we do, and the response has been amazing.

205
00:15:35,903 --> 00:15:40,795
Last week, just in time for tADWIG, our online public donations reached $100,000

206
00:15:40,914 --> 00:15:46,224
US, more than 1,750 individual donations from 48 countries,

207
00:15:46,704 --> 00:15:50,394
demonstrating how deeply individuals value BHL.

208
00:15:51,114 --> 00:15:55,524
This includes more than $2,200 US per month in recurring donations.

209
00:15:55,914 --> 00:16:00,564
Our current campaign, Become a BHL Sustainer, is trying to increase that reliable

210
00:16:00,654 --> 00:16:04,795
ongoing support. If you can afford a cup of coffee every week, please do join our

211
00:16:04,825 --> 00:16:06,174
BHL Sustains program.

212
00:16:07,825 --> 00:16:13,075
2026 does not only mark the beginning of BHL's new chapter, it is also BHL's 20th

213
00:16:13,164 --> 00:16:15,594
anniversary. Happy birthday, BHL.

214
00:16:16,854 --> 00:16:20,335
One of the ways we're celebrating this huge milestone is by showcasing the

215
00:16:20,364 --> 00:16:24,054
extraordinary breadth and history of BHL's collections and the people and the

216
00:16:24,114 --> 00:16:26,575
institutions that make BHL possible.

217
00:16:27,174 --> 00:16:30,414
Each post in this series celebrates the incredible treasures.

218
00:16:30,744 --> 00:16:33,265
Each one has been selected by a member of our community.

219
00:16:33,774 --> 00:16:36,835
So of course, they include rare books and historic field diaries.

220
00:16:37,254 --> 00:16:41,545
But they also showcase the treasure between the covers, such as the article data

221
00:16:41,575 --> 00:16:43,974
that makes our species descriptions discoverable.

222
00:16:44,394 --> 00:16:48,684
And our most recent post celebrates BHL as a repository of information about

223
00:16:48,744 --> 00:16:52,524
extinct species. I encourage you to check them out and follow along as our series

224
00:16:52,585 --> 00:16:54,234
unfolds through our anniversary year.

225
00:16:56,214 --> 00:16:58,374
We've come a long way across this bridge.

226
00:16:58,734 --> 00:17:02,484
Last year, there were times when we genuinely did not know if BHL would have a

227
00:17:02,575 --> 00:17:07,104
future. Today, we have a new foundation beneath us, new partnerships, new

228
00:17:07,164 --> 00:17:10,795
possibilities, new funding streams, new grants in the pipeline, and an

229
00:17:10,825 --> 00:17:14,725
extraordinary network of people and organizations holding us up.

230
00:17:15,475 --> 00:17:19,644
But I cannot stand here in front of you and say that BHL's future is secure.

231
00:17:20,154 --> 00:17:23,184
It isn't. We need to stop taking BHL for granted.

232
00:17:24,624 --> 00:17:28,374
A living bridge becomes stronger as more roots reach out, grow, intertwine, and

233
00:17:28,404 --> 00:17:34,374
together strengthen the whole. BHL now has the independence and autonomy to welcome

234
00:17:34,494 --> 00:17:37,854
ideas, partnerships, collaboration, and possibilities.

235
00:17:37,884 --> 00:17:42,414
So if you or your organization has expertise, resources, funding, or connections

236
00:17:42,444 --> 00:17:46,494
that could help make BHL stronger, please do reach out.

237
00:17:47,124 --> 00:17:51,324
BHL's future is not yet fixed, and that's what makes this time so exciting.

238
00:17:52,884 --> 00:17:56,484
We will be exploring BHL's future opportunities and possibilities throughout this

239
00:17:56,544 --> 00:18:00,384
week at tADWIG. Tomorrow afternoon, we have two back-to-back sessions and a

240
00:18:00,444 --> 00:18:04,914
dedicated BHL symposium where we'll explore some of those new partnerships,

241
00:18:05,064 --> 00:18:09,834
infrastructure, and ideas that are already reimagining and transforming BHL.

242
00:18:10,404 --> 00:18:13,914
And on Thursday, we have a hands-on workshop where we'll be exploring new

243
00:18:13,975 --> 00:18:18,084
approaches and tools to maximize the value of the Biodiversity Heritage Library.

244
00:18:20,082 --> 00:18:23,682
We invite you to join and help build VHL's future. Thank you for listening.

245
00:18:37,632 --> 00:18:40,272
We do have a minute for a question, if anyone has one.

246
00:18:42,732 --> 00:18:44,532
Or you can come and find Nicole later.

247
00:18:45,792 --> 00:18:47,892
Or come to our symposium or our workshop. I'll be there.

248
00:18:49,482 --> 00:18:50,472
Excellent. Thank you, Nicole.

249
00:18:56,442 --> 00:19:01,572
So, that was a powerful reminder that even our most trusted and beloved

250
00:19:01,782 --> 00:19:05,532
infrastructures still need constant care and reinvention.

251
00:19:06,342 --> 00:19:09,192
But building strong foundations is only half the story.

252
00:19:09,342 --> 00:19:12,882
We also need to get the data moving fast enough to matter.

253
00:19:13,513 --> 00:19:17,082
So our next speaker, Quentin Groom, will talk about exactly that,

254
00:19:18,312 --> 00:19:22,452
reducing the lag between observing something and being able to act on it.

255
00:19:23,112 --> 00:19:23,952
Thank you, Quentin.

256
00:19:29,263 --> 00:19:31,542
Thank you, Ellie, and thank you for inviting me to speak here.

257
00:19:31,602 --> 00:19:35,082
It's a wonderful opportunity. Yes.

258
00:19:35,562 --> 00:19:40,152
So we make an observation, and how long does it take before someone can actually

259
00:19:40,242 --> 00:19:44,082
act on it? And that's what I wanted to discuss with you in my 15 minutes.

260
00:19:45,252 --> 00:19:49,002
Just to make us completely clear, I think probably most people know in here, if you

261
00:19:49,062 --> 00:19:50,263
look at things like GBIF,

262
00:19:51,763 --> 00:19:55,662
this is a graph of the monthly observations globally in GBIF.

263
00:19:56,472 --> 00:20:01,482
You see the seasonal peaks, you see the exponential rise, and you see it drops off

264
00:20:01,542 --> 00:20:07,332
a cliff over the last two years because people haven't stopped making observations.

265
00:20:08,352 --> 00:20:10,932
They've just not mobilized their data yet.

266
00:20:12,372 --> 00:20:14,742
And, well, why does that matter?

267
00:20:16,452 --> 00:20:17,682
Hold on. I should mention one thing.

268
00:20:17,742 --> 00:20:19,092
If you go back and ex--

269
00:20:20,472 --> 00:20:25,812
Yeah, go back. And you extrapolate this up, you see this exponential growth, and

270
00:20:25,872 --> 00:20:28,122
you can kind of estimate how much that lag really is.

271
00:20:28,182 --> 00:20:31,752
It's obvious in the last couple of years, but in actual fact, it goes back quite a

272
00:20:31,812 --> 00:20:36,672
number of years, probably because people are still doing their PhDs and haven't

273
00:20:36,732 --> 00:20:41,263
published their data, or their herbarium hasn't published its data and things like

274
00:20:41,352 --> 00:20:46,513
this. So it's quite a long thing and accounts for tens of millions of observations

275
00:20:46,572 --> 00:20:47,082
as well.

276
00:20:49,182 --> 00:20:53,352
So I'm interested in invasive species. A lot of my group work on invasion biology.

277
00:20:54,162 --> 00:20:59,022
And any invasion biologist will know that you need to act on something quickly to

278
00:20:59,053 --> 00:21:00,672
have any chance of eradicating it.

279
00:21:01,182 --> 00:21:04,092
But there's other people working on biodiversity who are interested in things like

280
00:21:04,152 --> 00:21:08,772
pests, diseases, disease vectors, which don't want to work on the risks of two

281
00:21:08,832 --> 00:21:12,882
years ago, but want to work on the most up-to-date data as possible.

282
00:21:13,542 --> 00:21:17,803
But even if you're working in conservation, there's very little point, as we often

283
00:21:17,862 --> 00:21:23,352
seem to be, just charting the decline in species, when we actually want to find out

284
00:21:23,382 --> 00:21:27,582
which species are declining and act on them immediately and do something about that

285
00:21:27,612 --> 00:21:28,122
decline.

286
00:21:30,102 --> 00:21:35,201
So what often happens with biodiversity observations now is informal

287
00:21:35,263 --> 00:21:40,392
networks supply information. I see a gray squirrel in Brussels,

288
00:21:41,022 --> 00:21:46,662
and I email my friend who I know works on management of invasive species.

289
00:21:46,872 --> 00:21:48,702
I thought I saw a gray squirrel in Brussels.

290
00:21:48,763 --> 00:21:50,682
It may have been a red one, but I'm not really sure.

291
00:21:50,922 --> 00:21:56,562
And he writes to somebody else, "Maybe it was a gray squirrel," and then somebody

292
00:21:56,652 --> 00:21:59,862
who kills gray squirrels goes out and does something.

293
00:22:00,102 --> 00:22:04,752
And that works quite well at a local level, but it doesn't really scale.

294
00:22:05,803 --> 00:22:10,362
The other issue that's coming very rapidly is that although most of our

295
00:22:10,422 --> 00:22:13,992
observations on places like GBIF are created by humans,

296
00:22:15,282 --> 00:22:20,232
it's not going to be very long, it may be five years, that will get overtaken by

297
00:22:20,292 --> 00:22:24,492
all the data that's coming in from eDNA, from camera traps, from acoustic sensors,

298
00:22:25,212 --> 00:22:30,372
automated imaging, et cetera. And without that human in the loop, any invasive

299
00:22:30,462 --> 00:22:34,752
species in those data that are not being specifically looked for will just be not

300
00:22:34,842 --> 00:22:35,232
seen.

301
00:22:36,702 --> 00:22:40,572
So we have to find ways to alert people to invasions.

302
00:22:41,952 --> 00:22:47,112
So that normally happens by the species arriving.

303
00:22:47,832 --> 00:22:52,872
There's then a lag for detection. That then has to be reported.

304
00:22:53,172 --> 00:22:56,892
That needs to be alerted to all the people who need to do something, and then

305
00:22:56,952 --> 00:22:59,053
somebody goes out in the field and does something.

306
00:22:59,652 --> 00:23:03,342
Meanwhile, the species isn't just sitting there waiting to be killed.

307
00:23:04,212 --> 00:23:09,942
It establishes, it gets stronger, it spreads, and all the time, the costs of that

308
00:23:10,092 --> 00:23:14,772
eradication or management get higher and higher until it actually becomes not

309
00:23:14,832 --> 00:23:19,422
effective to be able to eradicate something. So where does all the time go?

310
00:23:20,562 --> 00:23:23,142
Well, it takes time to observe sometimes.

311
00:23:24,763 --> 00:23:29,832
Camera traps sit in the field for months with just an SD card picking up data.

312
00:23:31,092 --> 00:23:34,872
There's another lag on identification that I'll talk a bit about in a second.

313
00:23:35,682 --> 00:23:38,742
Digitization is often an issue with things like collections.

314
00:23:39,522 --> 00:23:42,792
A lot of work goes into then standardizing data that have been collected in all

315
00:23:42,852 --> 00:23:46,932
sorts of different ways, and validating that data, again, takes time.

316
00:23:46,992 --> 00:23:50,382
And ultimately, there's time associated with publication.

317
00:23:51,492 --> 00:23:56,172
Then, even after publication, people making alerts have to aggregate data.

318
00:23:56,232 --> 00:23:58,872
They analyze the data to find the important parts of it.

319
00:23:59,292 --> 00:24:03,222
They then send out alerts to the people who need to know, and action has to be

320
00:24:03,282 --> 00:24:03,763
taken.

321
00:24:06,342 --> 00:24:11,742
Oops, I skipped. So just taking one of those lags, the

322
00:24:11,803 --> 00:24:16,932
identification. These are data from iNaturalist, where I looked at how long does it

323
00:24:16,992 --> 00:24:17,412
take for

324
00:24:18,972 --> 00:24:24,942
a identification to become stable. So the top line, the blue line, is birds.

325
00:24:25,392 --> 00:24:27,432
Most of the time, about 50% of the time,

326
00:24:28,722 --> 00:24:34,212
the identification put on that bird is correct straight off as soon as someone puts

327
00:24:34,242 --> 00:24:38,682
it into iNaturalist, and then it takes several months, even years, before it gets

328
00:24:38,772 --> 00:24:44,472
close to its final point. Other taxa, the next one down are the mammals, the next

329
00:24:44,501 --> 00:24:47,352
one down are the insects, and right at the bottom are all the plants.

330
00:24:48,702 --> 00:24:53,232
Only 10% go in as the right identification right on day one, and it takes months

331
00:24:53,263 --> 00:24:55,182
before they reach an identification.

332
00:24:56,412 --> 00:24:57,912
So this is a problem,

333
00:24:59,292 --> 00:25:03,582
but this is a problem that identification is just difficult, and it's more

334
00:25:03,642 --> 00:25:05,832
difficult for difficult-to-identify things.

335
00:25:07,032 --> 00:25:12,042
Remote fieldwork means there's a built-in lag because of the difficulties, and

336
00:25:12,102 --> 00:25:16,782
taxonomic uncertainty builds in difficulties and builds in lags we find it hard to

337
00:25:16,842 --> 00:25:22,482
deal with. But there are also operational choices which are really a choice and

338
00:25:23,412 --> 00:25:27,342
not difficulties. Data sitting around doing nothing,

339
00:25:28,932 --> 00:25:33,912
people putting embargoes on data, systems aren't connected, so you can't publish

340
00:25:34,002 --> 00:25:39,612
easily from them, and waiting till a dataset is finished, whatever that

341
00:25:39,672 --> 00:25:40,033
means.

342
00:25:42,703 --> 00:25:42,912
So

343
00:25:44,922 --> 00:25:50,263
traditionally and still, we collect data, we identify it, we clean it, we validate

344
00:25:50,322 --> 00:25:51,822
it, analyze it, and we publish.

345
00:25:53,112 --> 00:25:58,752
But just like iNaturalist, just to some extent like our herbarium, although it

346
00:25:58,752 --> 00:26:04,092
doesn't work perfectly, what is better is to observe, to publish, to improve, to

347
00:26:04,182 --> 00:26:08,052
update, to improve, to update, to improve, to update continuously.

348
00:26:09,492 --> 00:26:13,393
What we don't necessarily have are ways to communicate with people that this is the

349
00:26:13,453 --> 00:26:16,572
raw data and this is getting better and this has been checked, and things like

350
00:26:16,632 --> 00:26:20,832
this, but we do have mechanisms to do this, and this allows users to get immediate

351
00:26:21,162 --> 00:26:22,182
access to data.

352
00:26:24,072 --> 00:26:28,722
If you're working on invasion biology, it doesn't matter if you have a few false

353
00:26:28,783 --> 00:26:32,442
positives. It matters if you have a lot of false positives, but you can do

354
00:26:32,533 --> 00:26:35,292
something with those data, and you can make a decision about whether you want to

355
00:26:35,382 --> 00:26:35,982
act on it.

356
00:26:37,632 --> 00:26:42,462
In our One Stop project, we are building a GBIF alert system.

357
00:26:43,092 --> 00:26:45,942
This is the work of Nico Nooy from the

358
00:26:47,472 --> 00:26:51,612
Binary Forest. That's his name, his company, probably. He's the programmer of this.

359
00:26:51,672 --> 00:26:57,582
If you're interested in invasion species alert, you can follow that QR code there

360
00:26:57,672 --> 00:26:59,682
and go to the website and take a look.

361
00:27:00,222 --> 00:27:05,172
This is open source code, and it allows people to build on top of GBIF a system

362
00:27:05,203 --> 00:27:08,862
that will alert people however they want to be alerted to whichever species they're

363
00:27:08,893 --> 00:27:13,152
interested in over a particular area they're interested in, and they can receive

364
00:27:13,212 --> 00:27:16,632
customized alerts on the invasive species they have.

365
00:27:16,962 --> 00:27:22,842
And I think Walter Kock is here. He's just made a system

366
00:27:22,872 --> 00:27:25,092
for Norway doing this. I don't think it's public yet.

367
00:27:25,422 --> 00:27:28,752
But if you're interested in this and want to find somebody who's actually a user of

368
00:27:28,813 --> 00:27:30,942
this, I think he's somewhere in the room

369
00:27:33,462 --> 00:27:39,072
So we heard this morning a nice introduction to the seven shortfalls that beset

370
00:27:39,132 --> 00:27:43,302
large-scale knowledge on biodiversity by Hortet et al.

371
00:27:43,962 --> 00:27:48,283
And I'm going to be rather bold by suggesting that they missed one,

372
00:27:49,813 --> 00:27:54,912
that is rival, because over 2,000 people who have cited this paper, and as you

373
00:27:54,942 --> 00:27:56,893
probably know, it's a very well-used paper.

374
00:27:57,791 --> 00:28:01,662
But they don't define a distinct shortfall associated with the timeliness.

375
00:28:02,143 --> 00:28:07,602
And I would argue that we aren't understanding biodiversity if we're only looking

376
00:28:07,632 --> 00:28:09,942
at biodiversity that was several years old.

377
00:28:10,332 --> 00:28:15,612
Timeliness is an important shortfall in what we do on work on biodiversity.

378
00:28:17,082 --> 00:28:22,453
So I would define that as a delay between the biodiversity observation being made

379
00:28:22,513 --> 00:28:26,263
and that information becoming accessible to be able to act on it.

380
00:28:27,283 --> 00:28:33,132
I got even bolder by naming that, and as any taxonomist knows, putting a

381
00:28:33,192 --> 00:28:36,192
name on something helps to have a conversation about it.

382
00:28:36,763 --> 00:28:42,162
And so I'm suggesting, and I've got to pronounce her name right, Wangari Maathai,

383
00:28:43,182 --> 00:28:47,352
is to be named after. She was a Kenyan zoologist.

384
00:28:47,592 --> 00:28:50,832
She won the Nobel Peace Prize, I guess, just here.

385
00:28:52,122 --> 00:28:54,042
And she was the founder of the Green Belt Movement.

386
00:28:54,402 --> 00:29:00,132
But she recognized that people in Kenya had information on the environmental

387
00:29:00,192 --> 00:29:04,872
decay in their area. She was also a politician, and she recognized the need to

388
00:29:04,902 --> 00:29:09,072
listen to these people, to get the data moving from those people to the people who

389
00:29:09,132 --> 00:29:15,072
could actually act on it. And so I suggest we have a shortfall for the

390
00:29:15,132 --> 00:29:19,722
timeliness of biodiversity data so that we can go out there and specifically work

391
00:29:19,813 --> 00:29:25,632
on shortening that timescale. So to summarize, publish soon, publish

392
00:29:25,722 --> 00:29:29,712
often, and improve continuously would be my mantra.

