00:33:24,042 --> 00:33:29,652
not just in form of citations, but literally for, in some cases, financial direct

455
00:33:29,712 --> 00:33:32,472
contribution to the data sources.

456
00:33:32,982 --> 00:33:33,283
And there are

457
00:33:36,042 --> 00:33:41,862
mechanisms that are being built now to do that, mostly driven by industry for

458
00:33:43,542 --> 00:33:47,742
nature data valuation, but it actually can work for other.

459
00:33:48,222 --> 00:33:54,192
What are your thoughts on using those mechanisms to speed up data access

460
00:33:54,313 --> 00:33:58,422
and alleviate this thought, say, you haven't published everything I could do from

461
00:33:58,453 --> 00:33:58,992
those yet?

462
00:34:00,612 --> 00:34:05,832
Yeah. I'm not sure I fully follow, but yeah, it doesn't make sense in the

463
00:34:06,672 --> 00:34:11,112
current system that you have grants for three years and you spend that money on

464
00:34:11,172 --> 00:34:14,712
data collection just for those three years, and we don't have this long-term

465
00:34:14,772 --> 00:34:16,993
funding for infrastructures to collect data.

466
00:34:17,022 --> 00:34:22,542
And obviously, you know about the neon data that is long-term collection.

467
00:34:22,932 --> 00:34:28,902
And we certainly have movements towards that in Europe, to have infrastructures for

468
00:34:28,993 --> 00:34:32,112
long-term data collection on biodiversity.

469
00:34:34,062 --> 00:34:35,953
Yeah, what are my thoughts on that? There's just so much.

470
00:34:38,592 --> 00:34:43,752
I would say we're still stuck on trying to observe everything, and that we need to

471
00:34:43,812 --> 00:34:49,062
focus. I think even though AI and

472
00:34:49,393 --> 00:34:53,682
automated systems will overtake human beings, we definitely need all those human

473
00:34:53,743 --> 00:34:59,112
being observations too, because they really fit an important niche.

474
00:35:00,612 --> 00:35:04,032
But we have to get much better at our indicators,

475
00:35:06,672 --> 00:35:10,152
and because we know what indicators we want to follow, we know what data collects

476
00:35:10,182 --> 00:35:14,712
in the first place. But then we also have the issue of the unknown unknowns, and

477
00:35:14,742 --> 00:35:18,582
that's where I think the human beings really come into play, is because I don't

478
00:35:18,643 --> 00:35:21,972
think AI yet is very good at the unknown unknowns.

479
00:35:22,062 --> 00:35:23,652
It just kind of tends to ignore them.

480
00:35:25,932 --> 00:35:27,402
I don't think that answered your question, but

481
00:35:27,582 --> 00:35:32,502
We can talk about it later. All right. Thank you, Quentin. Thank you.

482
00:35:40,212 --> 00:35:42,342
So we've had a reminder about the cost of delay,

483
00:35:44,623 --> 00:35:50,382
but then there's the other bit in there about the need to identify

484
00:35:50,442 --> 00:35:53,982
things. What is it? It's one thing to collect all that data, but do you know what

485
00:35:54,012 --> 00:35:57,192
it is? And then how do you get it into

486
00:36:00,462 --> 00:36:04,242
an infrastructure like GBIF or the Atlas of Living Australia, where I work?

487
00:36:05,232 --> 00:36:09,432
So Pam Cameron Sluiter, who's our next speaker, is going to take us inside one of

488
00:36:09,492 --> 00:36:14,202
the biggest untangling jobs in biodiversity informatics, which is to rebuild a

489
00:36:14,232 --> 00:36:17,143
national taxonomic backbone. Welcome, Pam.

490
00:36:25,393 --> 00:36:28,752
Hi, everybody. I'm guaranteed to stuff up the slides, so I'll just get that out of

491
00:36:28,752 --> 00:36:29,472
the way to start.

492
00:36:31,062 --> 00:36:35,592
I'd like to begin by acknowledging the traditional owners on the lands that Simon,

493
00:36:35,682 --> 00:36:41,623
my co-author, and I work on, the Ngunnawal people and

494
00:36:41,712 --> 00:36:46,368
the Wurrung- were Wurundjeri people of the Kulin nation.

495
00:36:46,428 --> 00:36:48,978
It always was Aboriginal land and always will be.

496
00:36:49,848 --> 00:36:53,118
We wish to acknowledge their elders, past and present, and to extend that

497
00:36:53,449 --> 00:36:56,598
acknowledgement to any First Nations people who might be here today.

498
00:36:58,068 --> 00:37:02,628
I'll be talking today about the use of taxonomic backbones to arrange data, how

499
00:37:02,658 --> 00:37:08,148
backbones require different rules to, say, taxonomic checklists,

500
00:37:08,718 --> 00:37:14,508
and how the ALA's gone about improving our backbone using the ALA as an

501
00:37:14,538 --> 00:37:15,199
example.

502
00:37:17,328 --> 00:37:23,208
So we are the largest publicly accessible data set in Australia with over 184

503
00:37:23,268 --> 00:37:26,358
million records and many thousands of users.

504
00:37:27,018 --> 00:37:32,388
Since its beginning, the taxonomic backbone of the ALA has been central to how we

505
00:37:32,718 --> 00:37:38,358
serve diversity data to users. Australia has a host of reasons for using a

506
00:37:38,418 --> 00:37:43,699
national species list, not only because of the high level of endemism in our biota,

507
00:37:43,788 --> 00:37:49,699
but also because there are differences in the taxonomy that we Australians use and

508
00:37:49,728 --> 00:37:52,818
what everybody else uses because we're just that contrary.

509
00:37:54,798 --> 00:37:58,458
We've got thousands of undescribed species only known by phrase names.

510
00:38:00,378 --> 00:38:04,668
We frequently in Australia use species names that end up in legislation and are

511
00:38:04,699 --> 00:38:10,578
therefore very hard to change. We have the risk posed by literally thousands

512
00:38:10,699 --> 00:38:15,048
of invasive species that are either in Australia or threatening to arrive.

513
00:38:15,738 --> 00:38:21,468
And there's the increasing importance of recognizing traditional ecological

514
00:38:21,558 --> 00:38:27,258
knowledge as part of the conservation and governance of biodiversity in Australia.

515
00:38:27,949 --> 00:38:32,388
The common thread in all this is a need for a simple common reference point for

516
00:38:32,449 --> 00:38:37,188
researchers, government, and industry, and that purpose has been served by many

517
00:38:37,248 --> 00:38:42,498
years by the National Species List of the Australian Biological Resources Study, or

518
00:38:42,588 --> 00:38:43,818
ABRS for short.

519
00:38:46,908 --> 00:38:52,758
So for much of the ALA's history, we've used the NSL, and the

520
00:38:52,818 --> 00:38:57,858
backbone of the taxonomy that we use in the ALA has been assembled what's been

521
00:38:57,918 --> 00:39:00,258
jokingly called the large taxon collider.

522
00:39:02,268 --> 00:39:06,588
This basically combines that ABRS National Species List with multiple other

523
00:39:06,678 --> 00:39:11,628
sources, including Catalog of Life, the New Zealand Organism Register, and also a

524
00:39:11,688 --> 00:39:14,628
host of other ad hoc changes from many other sources.

525
00:39:17,029 --> 00:39:22,008
The large taxon collider, in all its physical glory, provides a streamlined,

526
00:39:22,128 --> 00:39:27,768
automated process where taxonomic checklists are combined automatically with no

527
00:39:27,858 --> 00:39:33,738
human input. It's a very attractive and extremely efficient way of operating

528
00:39:33,798 --> 00:39:37,878
because it's got fairly minimal human intervention.

529
00:39:38,538 --> 00:39:43,368
However, the success of the collider requires that all input checklists that are

530
00:39:43,428 --> 00:39:44,868
coming from taxonomic checklists

531
00:39:47,449 --> 00:39:52,279
are consistent as possible in the taxonomic hierarchy they use, and that's because

532
00:39:52,699 --> 00:39:58,038
any variance in the hierarchy or authorship will generate errors and duplication as

533
00:39:58,097 --> 00:40:00,588
you produce the taxonomic backbone.

534
00:40:01,218 --> 00:40:04,488
This, of course, affects findability and data matching.

535
00:40:05,029 --> 00:40:09,378
The collider is dependent on incoming checklists having all possible name

536
00:40:09,438 --> 00:40:10,968
combinations for correct matching.

537
00:40:11,029 --> 00:40:15,438
And as we know, all taxonomic checklists are 100% complete, they never have any

538
00:40:15,498 --> 00:40:16,128
errors in them.

539
00:40:18,888 --> 00:40:22,398
But it's a never ending issue for us.

540
00:40:22,428 --> 00:40:27,199
So for example, to give it an Australian example, the common bent-wing bat, which

541
00:40:27,258 --> 00:40:32,598
is a rare and endangered species in Australia, despite its name, was misidentified

542
00:40:32,658 --> 00:40:37,428
for many years as a thing called Miniopterus schreibersii, before it was actually

543
00:40:37,488 --> 00:40:41,688
recognized as an endemic species, Miniopterus aereonoe.

544
00:40:42,199 --> 00:40:48,168
The problem is that because it wasn't actually a synonym, it was never included

545
00:40:48,228 --> 00:40:53,538
in the NSL. So what we had was a situation where we had an endangered

546
00:40:53,598 --> 00:40:58,968
species in the NSL, but the synonym by which most of the records in Australia were

547
00:40:59,058 --> 00:41:02,178
actually known were not actually connecting to anything.

548
00:41:03,588 --> 00:41:07,848
That's not a criticism of the NSL. Our colleagues in ABRS added it as soon as we

549
00:41:07,908 --> 00:41:12,678
found it. The issue is really until we started looking, it was simply unrecognized

550
00:41:12,798 --> 00:41:14,568
as an error in the collider process.

551
00:41:15,558 --> 00:41:20,898
The collider also assumes, and this is important, that the names are placed in a

552
00:41:20,958 --> 00:41:25,548
taxonomic checklist where they need to be for correct data matching, not where they

553
00:41:25,578 --> 00:41:27,468
need to be for taxonomic purposes.

554
00:41:27,738 --> 00:41:31,518
And it's absolutely not the same thing, and I want to make this clear.

555
00:41:32,238 --> 00:41:37,548
For example, having a misapplied name listed both against the taxon that it was

556
00:41:37,608 --> 00:41:42,828
misapplied to, as well as the place where it should be, which is relatively common

557
00:41:42,888 --> 00:41:46,608
in a taxonomic checklist, is a disaster for data matching.

558
00:41:47,268 --> 00:41:50,208
At worst, you're going to end up with a homonym, and at best, you're going to end

559
00:41:50,298 --> 00:41:54,168
up with the record in the wrong place in your taxonomic tree.

560
00:41:58,788 --> 00:42:03,348
Realistically, the idea of automatically building an error-free, human-free

561
00:42:03,588 --> 00:42:04,998
backbone is a fiction.

562
00:42:06,288 --> 00:42:08,958
There's rarely consistency between sources.

563
00:42:09,048 --> 00:42:13,878
There are always nearly errors and omissions, and the focus of taxonomic checklists

564
00:42:13,968 --> 00:42:18,199
is simply not the same as the focus of data aggregators or database managers.

565
00:42:18,798 --> 00:42:23,088
In fact, the inevitable consequence of using a large taxon collider without human

566
00:42:23,148 --> 00:42:27,408
intervention or quality control is demonstrated by what I've got up here.

567
00:42:28,128 --> 00:42:32,779
So this is a previous version of the ALA's taxonomic backbone from a few years ago.

568
00:42:33,648 --> 00:42:39,438
It shows at the very highest taxonomic levels exactly how taxonomic concepts are

569
00:42:39,558 --> 00:42:42,468
jumbled, the units aren't placing correctly,

570
00:42:44,178 --> 00:42:47,958
and there are simple duplications, and it's far worse at lower levels.

571
00:42:48,078 --> 00:42:52,098
And in this version of the taxonomic tree, which is where we started from, there

572
00:42:52,098 --> 00:42:57,258
were over 10 million records, which was 10% of the ALA's data holdings at the time,

573
00:42:57,558 --> 00:42:59,358
that simply weren't matching to anything.

574
00:43:01,098 --> 00:43:06,078
In one case, one scientific name had 78 different variations in the system.

575
00:43:06,708 --> 00:43:10,398
So is that important? Well, I'd argue if you're interested in AI,

576
00:43:11,628 --> 00:43:16,308
or in making your data either findable or reusable, it's pretty important.

577
00:43:16,818 --> 00:43:21,078
And if your focus is actually the best possible representation of biodiversity

578
00:43:21,168 --> 00:43:25,818
data, then there's far too much noise to isolate data quality issues or names

579
00:43:25,878 --> 00:43:28,788
matching issues. So how do you go about fixing it?

580
00:43:30,108 --> 00:43:34,788
Well, we took a systematic three-stage approach, through three successive

581
00:43:34,848 --> 00:43:40,758
backbones. In the first iteration, we looked at trying to

582
00:43:40,848 --> 00:43:44,538
make sure that the input checklists were as complete as possible.

583
00:43:45,048 --> 00:43:47,928
In the second iteration, we identified gaps and filled them.

584
00:43:48,408 --> 00:43:52,608
And in the last iteration, or the latest iteration, we focused on improving the

585
00:43:52,668 --> 00:43:57,768
backbone by looking at data mismatches and no matches, and I'll explain about that

586
00:43:57,798 --> 00:43:58,458
in a moment.

587
00:44:00,529 --> 00:44:06,408
At a high level, our core realization is, as I've already kind of indicated, and

588
00:44:06,468 --> 00:44:10,458
which seems really obvious in hindsight, that taxonomic checklists are not the same

589
00:44:10,518 --> 00:44:16,128
as taxonomic backbones. A backbone is there to display data against the best

590
00:44:16,188 --> 00:44:21,168
available name, not to correctly reflect all the taxonomic decisions and pathways

591
00:44:21,228 --> 00:44:23,838
that have led to that best available name.

592
00:44:24,648 --> 00:44:28,908
Therefore, taxonomic checklists are the essential basis of the system, but they

593
00:44:28,938 --> 00:44:33,138
require intervention by modification in order to display data correctly.

594
00:44:33,738 --> 00:44:38,568
This includes removing or repositioning possibly entirely valid names to improve

595
00:44:38,628 --> 00:44:44,118
data matching. Moreover, critical end uses, such as threatened species assessments

596
00:44:44,268 --> 00:44:49,248
or restricting sensitive species data, also require changes to the backbone.

597
00:44:49,638 --> 00:44:53,808
And to meet those cases, we might need to include informal synonyms such as

598
00:44:53,928 --> 00:44:58,488
spelling errors, populations, or even informal names that aren't actually part of

599
00:44:58,518 --> 00:44:59,838
the taxonomic framework.

600
00:45:03,288 --> 00:45:08,238
We've learned the four important issues for backbones are, firstly, to display

601
00:45:08,328 --> 00:45:10,068
data, you need a single hierarchy.

602
00:45:11,358 --> 00:45:15,888
I would argue that you don't actually need a scientifically valid hierarchy; you

603
00:45:15,978 --> 00:45:21,468
need a single hierarchy. You try to work towards a scientifically valid

604
00:45:21,529 --> 00:45:24,648
hierarchy, but what you need is something that avoids duplication.

605
00:45:25,518 --> 00:45:29,508
The second thing is there's always going to be a need to insert names that are

606
00:45:29,598 --> 00:45:35,568
absent from input checklists. In ALA, we prioritize species in legislation, such as

607
00:45:35,598 --> 00:45:39,768
threatened species, biosecurity species, or restricted access species.

608
00:45:41,598 --> 00:45:47,148
There's also a need to contentiously remove or move concepts that

609
00:45:47,208 --> 00:45:51,078
result in observation records going to the wrong best available name.

610
00:45:51,618 --> 00:45:53,568
And I've already talked about misapplication

611
00:45:55,830 --> 00:46:00,210
Misapplications, but there's also misidentifications and a host of other examples.

612
00:46:01,140 --> 00:46:06,601
Lastly, in certain instances, this may mean overriding the best current name in

613
00:46:06,660 --> 00:46:10,620
order to make sure that the data matches the commonly accepted name.

614
00:46:12,810 --> 00:46:16,741
The biggest innovation we've adopted is metric checking and feedback loops.

615
00:46:17,250 --> 00:46:18,660
We use Checklist Bank

616
00:46:19,950 --> 00:46:24,450
to test versions of our backbone using statistics on issues such as duplicates and

617
00:46:24,510 --> 00:46:27,210
orphan taxonomic units without children.

618
00:46:27,991 --> 00:46:32,190
We match draft taxonomic backbones against our data, and the process of matching

619
00:46:32,250 --> 00:46:36,991
data to our backbone enables us to identify what we call higher matches, where the

620
00:46:37,050 --> 00:46:41,430
name on an incoming observation will only match to a higher point in the taxonomy,

621
00:46:42,570 --> 00:46:46,200
or where there's no matches, i.e., the incoming name won't match to anything.

622
00:46:46,710 --> 00:46:51,840
But they're both basically really valuable guides as to where we need to focus our

623
00:46:51,870 --> 00:46:55,351
attention on from the perspective of improving the backbone.

624
00:46:56,280 --> 00:47:00,150
Most recently, we've extracted a complete set of parameters passed to names

625
00:47:00,241 --> 00:47:05,610
matching. This effectively gives us all 2.06 million names match combinations

626
00:47:06,601 --> 00:47:10,680
between our data and backbone, which basically drives the two processes that I've

627
00:47:10,741 --> 00:47:11,700
just talked about.

628
00:47:14,430 --> 00:47:17,640
So we're still using the code in the large taxon collider.

629
00:47:17,700 --> 00:47:22,530
So the large taxon collider still exists, but what we've done is stopped the

630
00:47:22,590 --> 00:47:26,790
colliding, and we've actually moved to a process where we control our inputs.

631
00:47:27,180 --> 00:47:31,920
We add our sources one at a time using the national species list as the core, and

632
00:47:32,010 --> 00:47:37,230
other elements are added bottom up to minimize duplicates and keep a single

633
00:47:37,290 --> 00:47:42,030
hierarchy. Feedback loops check every step and maintain quality control.

634
00:47:44,760 --> 00:47:47,190
And the proof, as they say, is in the pudding.

635
00:47:48,030 --> 00:47:53,130
So the total number of accepted taxonomic units over the last three years has

636
00:47:53,190 --> 00:47:59,070
reduced from 380,000 to 233,000, which as far as we can tell, is

637
00:47:59,760 --> 00:48:02,880
pretty close to the total number of named species in Australia.

638
00:48:03,721 --> 00:48:09,300
We have less than 1.5% of records affected now by names matching issues, and over

639
00:48:09,330 --> 00:48:13,950
10,000 species that previously had no data now have enough data to actually

640
00:48:15,450 --> 00:48:18,180
get them into public availability.

641
00:48:19,920 --> 00:48:24,840
Over 99% of species listed in government lists in Australia now correctly match to

642
00:48:25,170 --> 00:48:30,270
current scientific names. Moreover, and this has been actually really revealing,

643
00:48:30,330 --> 00:48:33,991
for the first time we've been able to generate reliable metrics on data gaps,

644
00:48:34,380 --> 00:48:36,840
taxonomically, spatially, and

645
00:48:38,190 --> 00:48:39,960
whatever the other one that is that I've forgotten

646
00:48:42,480 --> 00:48:45,721
that basically feeds into our data mobilization program.

647
00:48:45,780 --> 00:48:50,370
So we can actually target our digitization efforts and make suggestions to the

648
00:48:50,400 --> 00:48:55,380
collection sector and the ecological data sector in Australia as to where they

649
00:48:55,410 --> 00:48:58,200
should be focusing their efforts in terms of digitization.

650
00:48:59,130 --> 00:49:04,770
Lastly, we've been able to create a feedback loop back to ABRS to improve what the

651
00:49:04,830 --> 00:49:08,610
national species list by telling them where there are actually issues in the

652
00:49:08,910 --> 00:49:11,669
downstream system from a data quality perspective.

653
00:49:13,410 --> 00:49:17,101
Far be it for me to imply that we've solved everything, we absolutely haven't.

654
00:49:18,150 --> 00:49:23,130
So for that 1.5% of data that's still not matching, we know that at least a third

655
00:49:23,190 --> 00:49:26,280
of that can actually match, and we need to figure out why it's not.

656
00:49:26,971 --> 00:49:31,440
We need to come up with a solution to pro parte synonyms, which in most databases,

657
00:49:32,130 --> 00:49:36,630
what tends to happen is the record ends up on one synonym and not the others.

658
00:49:36,810 --> 00:49:41,910
So how do you actually alert to users that the record that they're looking for

659
00:49:41,971 --> 00:49:43,500
might be under another species?

660
00:49:44,730 --> 00:49:50,250
Homonyms remain our perennial pain, and we're working on a way to improve

661
00:49:50,280 --> 00:49:54,300
our way of handling genetic data as a growing data set.

662
00:49:54,721 --> 00:49:59,221
And lastly, we need a way of handling populations, or at least a term for groups

663
00:49:59,280 --> 00:50:04,020
below taxonomic resolution, which frequently feature in government species lists,

664
00:50:04,140 --> 00:50:06,540
either threatened species lists or sensitive lists.

665
00:50:09,900 --> 00:50:15,210
And just to get a little bit of AI in there, we generated an AI logo for taxonomic

666
00:50:15,270 --> 00:50:15,721
backbone.

667
00:50:19,320 --> 00:50:23,310
That's a post-death metal band called Taxonomic Backbone, in case you're wondering,

668
00:50:23,730 --> 00:50:28,230
you want the AI prompt. Thank you for TDWG for the opportunity to speak today.

669
00:50:28,560 --> 00:50:32,790
And in addition to our many colleagues in ALA, I'd also like to thank the ABRS

670
00:50:32,880 --> 00:50:37,500
team, the many taxonomists who contribute to the ecosystem in Australia, and

671
00:50:37,530 --> 00:50:42,991
particularly Bob Mesibov, who has single-handedly been a massive influence on our

672
00:50:43,050 --> 00:50:45,721
ability to deal with all of this. Thank you very much.

673
00:50:56,340 --> 00:51:01,080
Okay. I don't know if anyone can ask a question that would only give a 30-second

674
00:51:01,200 --> 00:51:03,991
answer, but if you think you can, then put your hand up.

675
00:51:05,760 --> 00:51:06,210
Quentin?

676
00:51:06,570 --> 00:51:07,920
When are T-shirts on sale?

677
00:51:11,400 --> 00:51:11,730
I hope

678
00:51:13,201 --> 00:51:13,290
so.

679
00:51:13,351 --> 00:51:17,280
Do you have one wish list for changes to the nomenclatural codes?

680
