01:08:49,254 --> 01:08:55,014
Okay. Let's move on. So we've heard about cleaning up half a

912
01:08:55,044 --> 01:09:00,655
million names, and we've heard about developing standards that we use to collect

913
01:09:00,684 --> 01:09:03,324
and share the data to the infrastructures.

914
01:09:04,374 --> 01:09:07,704
But it's another thing to turn that work into something a park ranger can actually

915
01:09:07,854 --> 01:09:13,794
use on the ground. So Judith Botha, who's joined us from South Africa, will show us

916
01:09:13,854 --> 01:09:17,995
what happens when that backbone work reaches the people making conservation

917
01:09:18,025 --> 01:09:22,194
decisions on the ground in South Africa's national parks. Thanks, Judith.

918
01:09:28,134 --> 01:09:30,204
Thanks, Ellie, and afternoon, everybody.

919
01:09:30,294 --> 01:09:33,594
So I'm going to take you guys on a quick safari to South Africa.

920
01:09:34,044 --> 01:09:36,084
So keep your hats on because it gets warm.

921
01:09:37,134 --> 01:09:42,025
So just to give you some background about what South African National Parks is.

922
01:09:42,474 --> 01:09:44,995
So really, it was established in 1976.

923
01:09:45,474 --> 01:09:49,104
It has quite a detailed mandate that you can see there on the left-hand side.

924
01:09:49,614 --> 01:09:53,485
But really the important part is that it's supposed to conserve, protect, control,

925
01:09:53,574 --> 01:09:59,094
and manage the national park systems and the biological diversity

926
01:09:59,184 --> 01:10:00,384
throughout South Africa.

927
01:10:02,064 --> 01:10:05,814
So currently we have 21 national parks across South Africa.

928
01:10:06,354 --> 01:10:10,824
The map nicely illustrates those in green, with the blue being the marine protected

929
01:10:10,914 --> 01:10:13,374
areas that we're also responsible for.

930
01:10:13,974 --> 01:10:19,314
So we manage approximately 4.4 million hectares of terrestrial national parks, as

931
01:10:19,374 --> 01:10:24,174
well as around 370,000 hectares of marine protected areas.

932
01:10:24,594 --> 01:10:28,974
So Kruger Park in the top right-hand corner there, that's where I'm based, even

933
01:10:29,004 --> 01:10:29,694
though I work

934
01:10:31,495 --> 01:10:33,623
over all of our parks in South Africa.

935
01:10:34,134 --> 01:10:38,094
It's roughly two million hectares, so that's about the size of Israel, okay?

936
01:10:38,214 --> 01:10:41,964
So to give you a little bit of an indication of the size.

937
01:10:42,204 --> 01:10:44,785
We also turned 100 years this year.

938
01:10:44,874 --> 01:10:49,614
So Kruger Park has been around for 100 years, and we hope to conserve it so that it

939
01:10:49,674 --> 01:10:55,104
can carry on for many more. So I think this map just nicely illustrates the wide

940
01:10:55,164 --> 01:10:58,524
range of biodiversity and biomes that we are responsible for.

941
01:10:59,004 --> 01:11:03,474
So it stretches across the marine, the terrestrial, and the aquatic environments,

942
01:11:03,535 --> 01:11:07,014
together with all the different biodiversity data associated with that.

943
01:11:07,704 --> 01:11:11,424
I saw earlier on, they were looking like on one of the presentations, it seemed

944
01:11:11,454 --> 01:11:17,304
like there was 176 or something animal species in Australia.

945
01:11:17,364 --> 01:11:18,174
Is that correct?

946
01:11:19,584 --> 01:11:23,964
157,000 I think you had on your first graph. Something like that.

947
01:11:24,024 --> 01:11:27,234
Anyway, we have about 67,000 animal species.

948
01:11:27,864 --> 01:11:32,304
But many of our groups are largely unknown, and also a lot of them have a high

949
01:11:32,364 --> 01:11:36,834
level of endemism. So obviously we are very interested in knowing about where they

950
01:11:36,924 --> 01:11:39,024
are and being able to conserve them.

951
01:11:39,474 --> 01:11:43,344
As Ellie mentioned, we have many rangers that work in our park, and we'd like to

952
01:11:43,944 --> 01:11:49,074
present them with tools that they can use to get snapshots and dashboards around

953
01:11:49,134 --> 01:11:51,984
the biodiversity data that they do have in their parks.

954
01:11:52,554 --> 01:11:54,924
So just moving on to what we do have.

955
01:11:55,014 --> 01:11:59,814
So the left-hand side shows that we have a number of scientists that are based in

956
01:11:59,874 --> 01:12:04,434
all of our national parks. So we have a lot of monitoring data that is collected.

957
01:12:04,854 --> 01:12:09,864
So this includes data on field sampling data, sensor data, image data, collar data,

958
01:12:10,374 --> 01:12:14,634
camera trap data. I think everybody in the audience kind of knows what all of that

959
01:12:14,964 --> 01:12:20,652
entails. It also then spans the three different biomes, marine,

960
01:12:20,742 --> 01:12:26,262
terrestrial, and aquatic. But we also have many other stakeholders and partners in

961
01:12:27,162 --> 01:12:31,633
our landscape that we are interested in getting their data as well to make sure

962
01:12:31,692 --> 01:12:34,782
that we can take evidence-based decisions.

963
01:12:35,232 --> 01:12:40,932
So our real problem came to how can we take all of this data and collate it so that

964
01:12:40,992 --> 01:12:46,662
we can come up with ways for rangers and park staff to quickly get a species list.

965
01:12:46,752 --> 01:12:52,572
Also, we are mandated to provide a management park every five years for every park,

966
01:12:53,052 --> 01:12:56,742
and in these management parks, we have to have updated species lists.

967
01:12:57,252 --> 01:13:02,262
So in the past, this was very laborious and very manual, so people went, you looked

968
01:13:02,292 --> 01:13:06,192
for all the literature, you took out all the species data, and then you then

969
01:13:06,252 --> 01:13:09,732
consolidated this and produced an annotated checklist.

970
01:13:10,242 --> 01:13:14,322
So we partnered with the Freshwater Research Centre, they've got a talk later in

971
01:13:14,383 --> 01:13:18,492
the week that you'll hear about, together with their technical partner, CartoSA,

972
01:13:18,582 --> 01:13:23,383
and we developed the BIMS, or the SANParks Biodiversity Information Management

973
01:13:23,472 --> 01:13:28,572
System. So in essence, what this is, it's a central platform to integrate

974
01:13:28,662 --> 01:13:34,122
biodiversity data across all the different areas and to

975
01:13:34,482 --> 01:13:38,142
help us make these evidence-based conservation decisions.

976
01:13:38,562 --> 01:13:42,402
So you'll see we have the taxon groups on the left-hand side there.

977
01:13:42,792 --> 01:13:47,353
So the ones that will be very familiar ones in the top line there, mammals, plants,

978
01:13:47,442 --> 01:13:52,422
birds. So our staff and our park rangers focus a lot of attention on those groups.

979
01:13:52,812 --> 01:13:56,112
But as you get down to the bottom line, you'll see there's things like fungi,

980
01:13:56,262 --> 01:14:01,362
algae, crustaceans, some very strange things, cnidarians, which I don't even know

981
01:14:01,422 --> 01:14:05,892
what they are, worms, bryozoans, et cetera.

982
01:14:06,252 --> 01:14:10,182
Obviously, we are interested in this biodiversity data as well, and we currently

983
01:14:10,242 --> 01:14:14,652
don't have any monitoring platforms to enable us to collect this data.

984
01:14:15,012 --> 01:14:19,812
So we would like to collect the data, get the data from other sources.

985
01:14:20,232 --> 01:14:24,732
So the system really has four features, the first one being the data harvesting and

986
01:14:24,792 --> 01:14:28,902
generating our checklist. The second one are these summary dashboards that people

987
01:14:28,962 --> 01:14:33,192
can pull up. Then we are also interested in looking at our red list species and how

988
01:14:33,222 --> 01:14:37,662
these trends change over time. And lastly, we can't look at species data without

989
01:14:37,722 --> 01:14:41,472
looking at the environment. So if you have your climate data together with your

990
01:14:41,532 --> 01:14:46,752
species data in one dashboard, it just makes it much quicker for people to get the

991
01:14:46,812 --> 01:14:52,362
data and interpret it. So on the left-hand side there on the top, you can see that

992
01:14:52,422 --> 01:14:56,952
we harvest both species data and occurrence data from GBIF directly.

993
01:14:57,402 --> 01:15:00,162
So our species data is harvested from a bigger area.

994
01:15:00,222 --> 01:15:04,722
We then have a much smaller area that contains our reserves, plus a buffer area and

995
01:15:04,782 --> 01:15:08,593
a botany catchment that we are interested to make sure that we are getting all the

996
01:15:08,682 --> 01:15:13,633
GBIF occurrence data. The bottom box shows that we enter then a whole lot of other

997
01:15:13,692 --> 01:15:18,822
data sources, which are unpublished literature, a lot of databases, published

998
01:15:18,883 --> 01:15:24,042
books, reports, theses. So we've got one or two staff members that basically all

999
01:15:24,072 --> 01:15:27,972
they do is they look at literature going back many years, and they actually take

1000
01:15:28,032 --> 01:15:31,482
that information out and upload it into our system.

1001
01:15:32,022 --> 01:15:36,732
Then the nice thing that we do use is we use the GBIF taxonomic backbone.

1002
01:15:36,822 --> 01:15:40,992
So luckily for this audience, I don't need to go into any detail around how that

1003
01:15:41,022 --> 01:15:45,343
works, but that's definitely a benefit for us and keeps us making sure that we're

1004
01:15:45,402 --> 01:15:48,642
only using the accepted names for our species.

1005
01:15:49,512 --> 01:15:54,012
So just to give you an example, so this is a Kruger-based example, and this is what

1006
01:15:54,162 --> 01:15:58,302
our BIMS looks like when a manager or a staff member will open it.

1007
01:15:58,782 --> 01:16:03,912
So as most web pages have, on the left-hand side, you'll see a number of filters.

1008
01:16:03,972 --> 01:16:07,722
So you can filter through from a particular taxonomic group.

1009
01:16:07,782 --> 01:16:13,152
It also has spatial temporal filters, and also then about red list status as well

1010
01:16:13,242 --> 01:16:17,952
as origin and endemism. So what I'm interested in doing here is to generate a bird

1011
01:16:18,012 --> 01:16:23,772
checklist for Kruger. So I've just highlighted the bird, and I've asked for

1012
01:16:23,892 --> 01:16:29,862
the spatial filter of Kruger, and then the dashboard gets generated, and

1013
01:16:30,012 --> 01:16:32,742
I can then download my annotated checklist.

1014
01:16:33,162 --> 01:16:35,383
So this is an Excel version of the checklist.

1015
01:16:35,442 --> 01:16:39,672
We also have this available that can be downloaded either as a PDF or CSV.

1016
01:16:40,152 --> 01:16:42,822
So you'll see there's categories at the top there.

1017
01:16:42,912 --> 01:16:48,162
So we have taxonomic information, then we have things around the species, the

1018
01:16:48,252 --> 01:16:53,052
status, the source, the spatial information, and also the record management.

1019
01:16:53,502 --> 01:16:58,032
So of particular importance are things like the GBIF coordinate uncertainty, as

1020
01:16:58,093 --> 01:16:59,742
well as the coordinate precision.

1021
01:17:00,252 --> 01:17:06,192
Some other handy columns is the number of records and when the last or the

1022
01:17:06,252 --> 01:17:11,142
most recent record was seen. So that can give us a good indication of is that

1023
01:17:11,232 --> 01:17:16,392
species still likely to be in the park, or was it something that was found many

1024
01:17:16,422 --> 01:17:18,672
years ago and probably isn't there anymore.

1025
01:17:19,062 --> 01:17:23,112
We also then have an include and exclude column, which allows us in the data that

1026
01:17:23,142 --> 01:17:28,212
we upload, if we know there has been a misidentification or we think it is, we can

1027
01:17:28,272 --> 01:17:33,672
then put in a to exclude, so that next time around when we develop the same species

1028
01:17:33,732 --> 01:17:38,202
list, we know that that was an erroneous record, and we don't need to include that

1029
01:17:38,262 --> 01:17:43,182
in our checklist anymore. So then just going on to the second feature, which is the

1030
01:17:43,272 --> 01:17:48,612
summary dashboards. So you can see there again, that's just the same graph or the

1031
01:17:48,672 --> 01:17:53,082
map that I had just now, the bird data, for Kruger National Park.

1032
01:17:53,412 --> 01:17:57,042
And then on the right-hand side, you can see there's some graphs that the rangers

1033
01:17:57,072 --> 01:18:02,172
can have a look at and download. So just to zoom into a little bit more detail,

1034
01:18:02,593 --> 01:18:07,152
you'll see this is one that shows endemism and origin, and then there's also graphs

1035
01:18:07,212 --> 01:18:10,512
that show conservation status as well.

1036
01:18:11,832 --> 01:18:16,422
So our red list species, what do we want to do with those and how do we track them?

1037
01:18:16,482 --> 01:18:21,222
Okay, so this is if we were interested in all the taxonomic groups within Kruger,

1038
01:18:21,672 --> 01:18:26,892
this feature gives us a graph and a summary table that shows us what percentage of

1039
01:18:27,012 --> 01:18:30,312
our species have the different red list status.

1040
01:18:30,612 --> 01:18:35,052
The graph at the bottom is the red list species index that our partner

1041
01:18:35,112 --> 01:18:37,122
organization, SANBI, has developed.

1042
01:18:37,603 --> 01:18:42,012
So you'll see the different colors on the graph of the different taxonomic groups.

1043
01:18:42,343 --> 01:18:48,312
So how the index works is that every time SANBI reevaluates the taxonomic group,

1044
01:18:48,522 --> 01:18:51,042
the status may or may not be changed.

1045
01:18:51,402 --> 01:18:55,482
And that's why, for example, you can see there's a big dip in the purple line.

1046
01:18:55,722 --> 01:18:58,482
That was in the year that it was reevaluated.

1047
01:18:58,843 --> 01:19:03,762
The closer the line is to zero means the more species in that particular taxonomic

1048
01:19:03,822 --> 01:19:09,282
group are close to extinction. So we would like our lines to be as close to one as

1049
01:19:09,343 --> 01:19:14,832
possible. There are several lines at the top of the graph that are just constantly

1050
01:19:14,922 --> 01:19:20,112
at one, and those are the taxonomic groups that haven't been evaluated.

1051
01:19:20,892 --> 01:19:23,802
So then lastly, just moving on to our climate dashboard.

1052
01:19:23,862 --> 01:19:28,452
So as I mentioned, it's really nice to have climatic data available if you are

1053
01:19:28,512 --> 01:19:30,883
looking at species distribution.

1054
01:19:31,332 --> 01:19:36,133
So this is just some of our climate stations that we have uploaded data.

1055
01:19:36,522 --> 01:19:39,822
So really easy for a ranger or park manager just to go in.

1056
01:19:39,883 --> 01:19:41,742
You can just find the station of interest.

1057
01:19:42,222 --> 01:19:47,622
You then get a series of graphs, you get some summary data, and you

1058
01:19:47,742 --> 01:19:51,012
also then get some graphs on the side.

1059
01:19:51,072 --> 01:19:55,242
You can see the bottom one, that's just from when the station began to when it

1060
01:19:55,332 --> 01:20:01,242
ended. It also has a temporal component, so you can pick out which years or

1061
01:20:01,272 --> 01:20:03,462
which dates you're interested in.

1062
01:20:03,792 --> 01:20:06,312
And then, of course, the data sets on the right-hand side.

1063
01:20:06,432 --> 01:20:11,982
Obviously, a lot of this data is partner data, so it has access

1064
01:20:12,042 --> 01:20:17,772
restrictions. But, for our SANParks staff, any of our SANParks colleagues can

1065
01:20:17,832 --> 01:20:23,562
actually get this information. So as this week is going around AI and automation,

1066
01:20:24,162 --> 01:20:28,332
if we can just pause for a minute and reflect on how both of these were used in

1067
01:20:28,362 --> 01:20:32,832
this talk. So firstly, obviously, my friend Chat helped me with some of the

1068
01:20:33,192 --> 01:20:38,112
prettier visuals. I do not have a very good brain on that side.

1069
01:20:38,202 --> 01:20:41,712
So all the prettiness comes from Chat, but I promise it's all my work.

1070
01:20:42,192 --> 01:20:44,742
So just around the data harvesting and the checklist.

1071
01:20:45,072 --> 01:20:49,782
So as I mentioned, the data is harvested on a period specific to

1072
01:20:49,843 --> 01:20:54,552
particular taxonomic groups directly from GBIF itself.

1073
01:20:55,002 --> 01:20:59,802
Then we also have large data sets that are running all the time.

1074
01:20:59,922 --> 01:21:04,843
So we have a lot of staff in the field that have collection devices.

1075
01:21:04,902 --> 01:21:08,322
These are then entered with different field platforms.

1076
01:21:08,353 --> 01:21:12,912
It goes to some software package. We then write scripts and automations to make

1077
01:21:12,942 --> 01:21:17,430
sure that we're cleaning that data and that they are ready then for To be entered

1078
01:21:17,490 --> 01:21:22,800
into our platforms. Then the assimilation of the species information across the

1079
01:21:22,830 --> 01:21:26,370
multiple sources, that's all done by automation.

1080
01:21:26,731 --> 01:21:31,110
Our checklist annotations, and then, of course, importantly, our species taxonomy

1081
01:21:31,140 --> 01:21:33,600
is verified against the backbone.

1082
01:21:33,990 --> 01:21:39,540
Summary databases, many of the information that we get is automatically

1083
01:21:39,600 --> 01:21:41,910
assigned values from the particular website.

1084
01:21:42,270 --> 01:21:47,191
So all our citers information is drawn directly from there, so nobody has to

1085
01:21:47,760 --> 01:21:51,810
re-input that. And then, of course, the generations of the dashboards and the

1086
01:21:51,870 --> 01:21:54,330
summaries is all done automatically.

1087
01:21:55,110 --> 01:21:58,050
Pretty much the same thing with our Red List Species index.

1088
01:21:58,410 --> 01:22:02,340
So we are automatically assigning the values as they come off the different

1089
01:22:02,400 --> 01:22:06,510
websites. Then we have this generation of dashboards and summaries.

1090
01:22:06,900 --> 01:22:10,050
And then lastly, the generation of the Red List index.

1091
01:22:10,560 --> 01:22:13,890
Quite similar for our climate summaries and downloads.

1092
01:22:14,250 --> 01:22:19,441
Once again, this automation of these summaries and dashboards so that our staff

1093
01:22:19,530 --> 01:22:24,600
don't have to go and reinvent the wheel every time they would like to get a summary

1094
01:22:24,660 --> 01:22:29,460
done. So then just in closing-- oh, sorry, hold on. There we are.

1095
01:22:29,910 --> 01:22:35,070
So in conclusion, automation and AI have played a large role in ensuring that our

1096
01:22:35,130 --> 01:22:40,500
large datasets are integrated and that they ensure us to improve our evidence-based

1097
01:22:40,590 --> 01:22:42,930
decision making. So just to

1098
01:22:44,191 --> 01:22:49,770
move on to my thank yous, I'd like to just say a big thank you to our

1099
01:22:49,860 --> 01:22:53,519
funders. So we had two rounds of funding in this project.

1100
01:22:53,580 --> 01:22:58,320
First, the Jaris Biodiversity Foundation gave us a substantial grant, and then the

1101
01:22:58,441 --> 01:23:04,050
AFD or the French Development Agency, our partners, Freshwater Research

1102
01:23:04,380 --> 01:23:09,000
Commission, as well as Cartoza. Without their big brains, we would never have got

1103
01:23:09,030 --> 01:23:13,500
this off the ground. And then lastly, to the funders who've made it possible for me

1104
01:23:13,560 --> 01:23:18,210
to be here, of course, SANParks, but then also the Science, Technology and

1105
01:23:18,270 --> 01:23:24,150
Innovation Department from South Africa, as well as the National Science Collection

1106
01:23:24,210 --> 01:23:26,040
Facility. So thank you for listening.

1107
01:23:26,130 --> 01:23:28,890
If anybody is interested, I will be around for the week.

