platforms focused on optics and spectroscopy.

1248
01:35:04,668 --> 01:35:08,448
But this presentation actually deals with quite possibly my favorite vein of

1249
01:35:08,508 --> 01:35:13,368
information science, which is what I'm getting my PhD in, and that is information

1250
01:35:13,459 --> 01:35:18,468
ecosystems. And it came really from a place of reflecting on my time living and

1251
01:35:18,528 --> 01:35:20,268
working in Yellowstone National Park.

1252
01:35:20,988 --> 01:35:25,518
And the question that ended up emerging was: what if the way ecosystems respond to

1253
01:35:25,578 --> 01:35:29,598
disruption is the way we should design the systems that manage scientific

1254
01:35:29,628 --> 01:35:34,188
knowledge? So this presentation actually begins with an analogy and a story about a

1255
01:35:34,218 --> 01:35:38,358
woman named Molly Beatty. She was the director of the US Fish and Wildlife Service

1256
01:35:38,418 --> 01:35:43,278
beginning in 1993, and she had to make a decision that helped reshape one of the

1257
01:35:43,338 --> 01:35:46,608
most studied ecosystems in the world, which is Yellowstone National Park.

1258
01:35:47,388 --> 01:35:51,738
And Beatty was a scientist, a lawyer, a public servant, and when she assumed

1259
01:35:51,799 --> 01:35:55,188
leadership, she inherited a system that was already deep in conflict.

1260
01:35:55,698 --> 01:36:01,068
Ranchers, scientists, policymakers, federal agencies, local communities, and

1261
01:36:01,128 --> 01:36:05,959
landscapes were all entangled in competing priorities, and she stood at that

1262
01:36:05,988 --> 01:36:10,668
decision point with her team, and that decision was wolves.

1263
01:36:11,268 --> 01:36:15,198
So by the early 20th century, wolves had been systematically eradicated from

1264
01:36:15,228 --> 01:36:20,299
Yellowstone, and the last resident packs disappeared in the 1920s through hunting

1265
01:36:20,358 --> 01:36:24,588
and predator control programs, reflecting an assumption at the time that large

1266
01:36:24,618 --> 01:36:26,148
predators were inherently harmful.

1267
01:36:26,718 --> 01:36:30,408
So what followed ended up not being a system that was frozen in the wolves'

1268
01:36:30,528 --> 01:36:36,258
absence, but one where elk, bison, vegetation, other predators, climate, fire,

1269
01:36:36,348 --> 01:36:40,518
hydrology, human management, and more continued interacting.

1270
01:36:41,209 --> 01:36:43,398
Removing the wolf had not removed complexity.

1271
01:36:43,518 --> 01:36:47,748
It had just removed one set of relationships from an already complex system.

1272
01:36:48,468 --> 01:36:52,607
And restoring these relationships meant returning an apex predator to a landscape

1273
01:36:52,638 --> 01:36:57,738
from which it had been absent for roughly 70 years while under extreme scrutiny and

1274
01:36:57,828 --> 01:37:02,478
opposition. Understandably so, ranchers feared for their livestock.

1275
01:37:02,988 --> 01:37:04,998
Communities questioned the necessity.

1276
01:37:05,538 --> 01:37:10,368
Scientists could anticipate some outcomes, but they could not specify precisely how

1277
01:37:10,608 --> 01:37:13,098
an ecosystem this complex would respond.

1278
01:37:13,758 --> 01:37:19,698
But nevertheless, in January 1995, 14 wolves from Canada entered

1279
01:37:19,728 --> 01:37:24,528
Yellowstone, with 17 following the next year, and that was 14 animals initially

1280
01:37:24,618 --> 01:37:29,088
against 2.2 million acres with no precise model of what was to follow.

1281
01:37:30,978 --> 01:37:35,868
And what ended up following became one of the most iconic and maybe most debated

1282
01:37:35,959 --> 01:37:40,308
examples of a trophic cascade, which is the process where changes at one trophic

1283
01:37:40,368 --> 01:37:43,549
level can propagate indirectly through others.

1284
01:37:44,148 --> 01:37:47,209
But trophic cascades are not simple chains of cause and effect.

1285
01:37:47,388 --> 01:37:53,118
They unfold within networks. Their effects can be nonlinear, spatially uneven,

1286
01:37:53,778 --> 01:37:59,658
delayed, amplified, weakened, and redirected by processes

1287
01:37:59,748 --> 01:38:01,158
occurring elsewhere in the system.

1288
01:38:01,818 --> 01:38:05,209
So wolves did not simply return and reduce elk populations.

1289
01:38:05,959 --> 01:38:10,188
Their return altered predation pressure within a system that also contained other

1290
01:38:10,248 --> 01:38:13,698
components. Elk abundance and distribution had changed.

1291
01:38:13,998 --> 01:38:18,228
Browsing pressure also changed. And aspen and willow recovered in some places while

1292
01:38:18,288 --> 01:38:22,068
not in others. And beaver populations increased, and bison expanded.

1293
01:38:22,459 --> 01:38:26,209
And to this day, scientists continue debating the relative importance of these

1294
01:38:26,268 --> 01:38:32,028
pathways. And what emerged was not the simple chain of wolves changed elk,

1295
01:38:32,209 --> 01:38:35,268
elk released vegetation, vegetation restored the rivers.

1296
01:38:35,568 --> 01:38:40,308
There was something infinitely more interesting happening, and that is a network of

1297
01:38:40,368 --> 01:38:44,508
interacting responses whose strength and direction varied across space and time.

1298
01:38:45,228 --> 01:38:48,888
So no individual wolf contained a blueprint for Yellowstone.

1299
01:38:49,068 --> 01:38:52,878
They didn't know how to restore an aspen stand, and no elk knew that it was

1300
01:38:52,908 --> 01:38:55,098
participating in a trophic cascade.

1301
01:38:55,818 --> 01:39:00,438
System-level change emerged from interactions among these components because

1302
01:39:00,468 --> 01:39:04,518
Yellowstone is not only an ecological story, but also a story of systems.

1303
01:39:05,028 --> 01:39:09,348
So what Beatty and her team helped enable, and what ecologists have spent the last

1304
01:39:09,528 --> 01:39:14,388
30 years documenting, is demonstration that complex systems do not necessarily

1305
01:39:14,418 --> 01:39:17,898
respond to interventions in entirely predictable ways.

1306
01:39:18,588 --> 01:39:23,778
Change one relationship, and other relationships respond. The system reorganizes.

1307
01:39:25,314 --> 01:39:29,544
And an ecosystem, in many ways, is information unfolding in real-time.

1308
01:39:30,144 --> 01:39:34,704
So the movement of elk, the growth of an aspen, or the shifting of a riverbank are

1309
01:39:34,734 --> 01:39:39,294
physical processes, but each event in the present becomes part of the informational

1310
01:39:39,444 --> 01:39:43,254
substrate through which we understand the system in the future via data.

1311
01:39:44,004 --> 01:39:47,484
And the same is true of the information systems that we build to study

1312
01:39:47,514 --> 01:39:50,304
biodiversity. They operate up to global scales.

1313
01:39:50,694 --> 01:39:54,414
They integrate observations across institutions, disciplines, technologies, and

1314
01:39:54,504 --> 01:39:59,484
time. And the challenge is not only storing the data, although that is non-trivial.

1315
01:39:59,995 --> 01:40:03,684
The challenge is enabling information to move through systems that it was not

1316
01:40:03,774 --> 01:40:05,664
originally designed to operate within.

1317
01:40:06,384 --> 01:40:10,314
So different institutions, different standards, different tools, different

1318
01:40:10,374 --> 01:40:14,304
histories, and priorities, each can occupy something like its own niche.

1319
01:40:14,754 --> 01:40:19,434
And each may be internally coherent, but collectively, they form an information

1320
01:40:19,524 --> 01:40:23,934
ecosystem, which is interdependent, loosely coupled, heterogeneous, and only

1321
01:40:24,024 --> 01:40:28,374
partially coordinated. So once again, that question that ended up emerging is, what

1322
01:40:28,404 --> 01:40:32,424
would it mean to design information systems with some of the properties we observe

1323
01:40:32,454 --> 01:40:36,234
in complex ecological systems? In an ecosystem,

1324
01:40:36,294 --> 01:40:42,294
components

1325
01:40:42,354 --> 01:40:43,884
matter because of their interactions.

1326
01:40:44,094 --> 01:40:48,294
A change in one place can become an event to which something elsewhere responds.

1327
01:40:48,774 --> 01:40:53,245
And system-level behavior emerges not from any single component, but the

1328
01:40:53,274 --> 01:40:56,064
relationships among them. So

1329
01:40:57,444 --> 01:41:01,344
wolves mattered not simply because wolves were present, but because their presence

1330
01:41:01,404 --> 01:41:04,374
restored interactions that had been absent from the system.

1331
01:41:05,094 --> 01:41:09,414
Similarly, data does not become valuable simply because it exists in a repository

1332
01:41:09,474 --> 01:41:13,495
or an aggregator. Its greater value emerges through interaction.

1333
01:41:13,974 --> 01:41:17,364
A new dataset enters an infrastructure. That arrival becomes an event.

1334
01:41:17,904 --> 01:41:20,574
A workflow is triggered. A product is updated.

1335
01:41:20,934 --> 01:41:24,954
A model can be recalibrated, and now an analysis has become possible.

1336
01:41:25,434 --> 01:41:30,204
And other datasets can now acquire new context, and those outputs themselves can

1337
01:41:30,234 --> 01:41:34,434
become new events. So information begins to cascade through the system.

1338
01:41:35,064 --> 01:41:38,694
And this perspective is what motivates the architectural approach taken in my

1339
01:41:38,754 --> 01:41:43,374
research, which is event-driven modular systems informed by microservices

1340
01:41:43,464 --> 01:41:48,234
architecture paradigms. In these systems, components are designed to be loosely

1341
01:41:48,294 --> 01:41:52,644
coupled but highly responsive. And rather than enforcing one monolithic pipeline in

1342
01:41:52,674 --> 01:41:57,384
which every component must change together, microservices allow functions such as

1343
01:41:57,444 --> 01:42:02,904
data ingestion, processing, analysis, transformation, and dissemination to operate

1344
01:42:02,995 --> 01:42:08,484
independently while communicating through well-defined interfaces, protocols, and

1345
01:42:08,544 --> 01:42:11,634
responding dynamically to events in the larger system.

1346
01:42:12,294 --> 01:42:17,094
So no individual service contains a blueprint for the entire information ecosystem.

1347
01:42:17,514 --> 01:42:21,114
System-level behavior emerges through coordination among them.

1348
01:42:21,745 --> 01:42:26,755
So this architecture is scalable not only computationally but sociotechnically.

1349
01:42:27,325 --> 01:42:31,374
So new components can be introduced without requiring the entire system to be

1350
01:42:31,404 --> 01:42:35,634
redesigned, and different organizations can maintain their own services while

1351
01:42:35,664 --> 01:42:40,015
participating in a shared information ecosystem through standards, interfaces, and

1352
01:42:40,044 --> 01:42:41,034
event exchanges.

1353
01:42:42,265 --> 01:42:47,844
In this way, microservices enable a form of coordination without requiring the

1354
01:42:47,874 --> 01:42:49,825
burden of complete centralization.

1355
01:42:50,784 --> 01:42:54,534
But loosely coupled, by no means, is structureless.

1356
01:42:55,015 --> 01:42:58,974
There remains a necessary layer of shared structure, typically through standards

1357
01:42:59,005 --> 01:43:03,174
and governance mechanisms that allow independent components to understand and

1358
01:43:03,234 --> 01:43:08,575
respond to one another. So this shared structure does not eliminate difference.

1359
01:43:08,995 --> 01:43:12,505
It creates conditions under which difference can coexist productively.

1360
01:43:14,064 --> 01:43:18,084
So this is also not entirely foreign to biodiversity informatics.

1361
01:43:18,624 --> 01:43:23,005
In many ways, Darwin Core already allows our sociotechnical infrastructure to

1362
01:43:23,034 --> 01:43:27,894
behave like a modular system. A natural history museum, the NEON Biorepository,

1363
01:43:28,164 --> 01:43:33,745
iNaturalist, and NASA collect fundamentally different forms of evidence, and they

1364
01:43:33,894 --> 01:43:38,064
operate under different institutional missions, technologies, and definitions of

1365
01:43:38,124 --> 01:43:43,075
expertise. They do not by any means need to adopt the same internal database

1366
01:43:43,134 --> 01:43:47,784
protocols, workflows, or organizational models in order to contribute to a shared

1367
01:43:47,844 --> 01:43:53,184
information ecosystem. Instead, Darwin Core provides something like a data

1368
01:43:53,245 --> 01:43:58,464
contract where institutions can map locally managed information into a

1369
01:43:58,495 --> 01:44:02,005
vocabulary that other components of the ecosystem can interpret.

1370
01:44:02,604 --> 01:44:07,644
GBIF can then aggregate and expose those records so that researchers can query

1371
01:44:07,705 --> 01:44:09,265
across institutional boundaries.

1372
01:44:09,894 --> 01:44:14,154
And no single institution needs to be omniscient of the stack that is happening.

1373
01:44:14,904 --> 01:44:19,554
So the museum can remain a museum. NEON can maintain its own sampling and

1374
01:44:19,584 --> 01:44:24,505
repository protocols. iNaturalist can retain its community-based novel, and their

1375
01:44:24,564 --> 01:44:28,255
differences do not have to disappear for the information to become interoperable.

1376
01:44:28,944 --> 01:44:34,044
But there is power in making implicit behaviors such as these explicit in

1377
01:44:34,104 --> 01:44:38,364
architecture. When we recognize something like Darwin Core as part of connective

1378
01:44:38,424 --> 01:44:41,844
infrastructure, we can design more intentionally around it.

1379
01:44:42,534 --> 01:44:46,015
We can ask where translation occurs, which assumptions are embedded in those

1380
01:44:46,075 --> 01:44:50,394
mappings, and what provenance must be preserved, and who is responsible for

1381
01:44:50,424 --> 01:44:52,854
maintaining the interface between systems.

1382
01:44:53,304 --> 01:44:57,564
And this allows for biodiversity informatics to remain institutionally diverse

1383
01:44:57,624 --> 01:45:01,674
while still functioning in a larger interconnected system.

1384
01:45:02,304 --> 01:45:05,814
And as I said in the beginning, in my work, I scale these concepts

1385
01:45:07,434 --> 01:45:12,294
to be connected across disciplines while also allowing new additions with far less

1386
01:45:12,354 --> 01:45:18,024
friction. So at NASA, before anybody really sees the data, it goes through about

1387
01:45:18,084 --> 01:45:19,884
three levels. So that's what the L's are.

1388
01:45:20,124 --> 01:45:25,075
L0 is the raw observation and digital numbers that you get off of the sensor, and

1389
01:45:25,224 --> 01:45:29,304
L1 is the calibrated radiance, and then L2 is reflectance, which is what people

1390
01:45:29,394 --> 01:45:31,314
usually use to get derived products.

1391
01:45:32,094 --> 01:45:36,714
So for example, if NASA's remote sensing data that I work with becomes part of GBIF

1392
01:45:36,804 --> 01:45:40,884
information, NASA would first need to go through all of this before it even gets to

1393
01:45:40,944 --> 01:45:41,634
Darwin Core.

1394
01:45:43,614 --> 01:45:48,654
So this allows for including NASA's remote sensing data as an addition and not an

1395
01:45:48,714 --> 01:45:53,904
entire paradigm change, because we would not be putting satellite imagery into GBIF

1396
01:45:53,995 --> 01:45:57,834
via Darwin Core. We would be using Darwin Core to represent the biodiversity

1397
01:45:57,894 --> 01:45:59,874
observations derived from that imagery.

1398
01:46:00,745 --> 01:46:04,734
So the challenge then is not to build systems that eliminate complexity.

1399
01:46:05,124 --> 01:46:08,184
It's to build systems capable of living with complexity.

1400
01:46:08,934 --> 01:46:12,265
Yellowstone did not become a perfectly balanced system following wolf

1401
01:46:12,325 --> 01:46:16,194
reintroduction, but it did become a different system with new dynamics.

1402
01:46:16,765 --> 01:46:19,374
Conflict between conservation and land use persists.

1403
01:46:19,825 --> 01:46:22,584
Management decisions remain very much contested.

1404
01:46:23,034 --> 01:46:26,964
Ecological relationships continue changing, and even our scientific interpretation

1405
01:46:27,024 --> 01:46:30,924
of what happened has changed as decades of new observations have accumulated.

1406
01:46:31,674 --> 01:46:33,834
The intervention did not resolve complexity.

1407
01:46:34,164 --> 01:46:37,015
It changed the conditions under which complexity unfolded.

1408
01:46:37,674 --> 01:46:41,064
And this is not a failure. This is what system-level change looks like.

1409
01:46:41,424 --> 01:46:44,154
And the same is true of infrastructures developed in my work.

1410
01:46:44,575 --> 01:46:48,594
Improved data pipelines do not eliminate debates over data ownership, standards,

1411
01:46:48,654 --> 01:46:53,034
governance, or interpretation, and new architectures do not dissolve institutional

1412
01:46:53,094 --> 01:46:57,024
boundaries. They instead change the conditions under which those boundaries

1413
01:46:57,084 --> 01:47:01,374
interact, and they enable new forms of exchange, collaboration, response, and

1414
01:47:01,464 --> 01:47:05,995
adaptation. Because the goal has never been to solve the information ecosystem,

1415
01:47:06,534 --> 01:47:09,984
it's to create conditions under which the information ecosystem can continue to

1416
01:47:10,734 --> 01:47:15,834
evolve. Molly Beatty, once again, and her team did not fix Yellowstone, but what

1417
01:47:15,864 --> 01:47:18,955
she did was help restore a relationship that had been removed from it.

1418
01:47:19,495 --> 01:47:25,374
Some effects cascaded, others weakened, some occurred only locally, and some became

1419
01:47:25,404 --> 01:47:29,544
visible only after years or decades of observation. And that's the model.

1420
01:47:30,234 --> 01:47:34,765
Not a static and perfect and complete system, and not a system in which every

1421
01:47:34,854 --> 01:47:39,384
outcome must be specified in advance, but systems capable of cascading and

1422
01:47:39,444 --> 01:47:41,484
reorganizing as conditions change.

1423
01:47:41,964 --> 01:47:45,474
The lesson of Yellowstone is not that one wolf can change a river, but that

1424
01:47:45,505 --> 01:47:49,674
restoring infrastructural relationships can change what an entire system is capable

1425
01:47:49,734 --> 01:47:55,224
of doing. And my question for my PhD, perhaps obsession if I'm doing it for a

1426
